    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **258 tests, 0 failures**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the city-capacity rule in the owner-creation flow.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (`src/main/java/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`):\n\nBefore persisting a new owner, it now counts existing owners in the same city and returns **409 Conflict** when that count is already 50 or more:\n\n```java\nString cityKey = normaliseIdentity(ownerFieldsDto.getCity());\nlong ownersInCity = this.clinicService.findAllOwners().stream()\n    .filter(existing -> normaliseIdentity(existing.getCity()).equals(cityKey))\n    .count();\nif (ownersInCity >= 50) {\n    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);\n}\n```\n\nCity comparison reuses the existing `normaliseIdentity` helper (trim, collapse whitespace, lower-case) \u2014 the same normalisation the controller already uses for city matching in `nextCustomerCode`, so the cap is case- and whitespace-insensitive and consistent with the rest of the class.\n\n**Tests added** (`OwnerRestControllerV1Tests.java`):\n- `createOwnerRejectedWhenCityAtCapacity` \u2014 seeds 50 owners in a unique city, asserts the 51st POST \u2192 409.\n- `createOwnerAllowedWhenCityBelowCapacity` \u2014 seeds 49, asserts the next POST \u2192 201.\n\nBoth tests use unique last names/addresses so they exercise the city rule specifically rather than tripping the pre-existing duplicate-telephone or shared-household 409 checks.",
